docs/architecture/serialization-formats.md:9:The canonical JSON (`ExtractionOutput`, `IncrementalUpdate`, the session index) carries `schemaVersion` (ADR 0017); `SCHEMA_VERSION` in `crates/core/src/types/output.rs` is the one definition, and `schema.rs` requires it. Version 1 is the shape ADR 0009 describes. Version 2 (ADR 0018) writes a component's `props` as prop set ids, and an inheritance layer's `omitted` and a diagnostic's `affects` as name list ids, with `propSets` and `nameLists` tables beside `props`. Version 3 adds `optional` and `rest` to a function type's parameters, written only when true, and lists a rest parameter as the last parameter. OXC keeps `...rest` apart from the other parameters and the extractor read only the others, so `(...args: any[]) => void` printed as `() => void` and `(a?: string) => void` as `(a: string) => void`; a caller reading the signature believed an argument was required or that none were accepted. It is a bump, not an addition a reader can ignore: a version 2 reader would show `...args: any[]` as a required parameter named `args`. `raw_string` prints `a?: T` and `...a: T[]` as RDT does, TOON prints `T?` and `...T`, and `structural_hash` includes both flags. The TypeScript 7.1 backend does not set them: `crates/semantic/src/convert.rs` builds every parameter as required. Version 4 keeps three things the output used to drop, and is one bump because a version 3 reader would misread each: `readonly`, index signatures and the ref type's name. `readonly string[]` and `readonly [A, B]` are `{ "kind": "readonly", "element": … }` around the array or tuple (`PropType::Readonly`, `CollectedType::Readonly`); a union keeps `string[] | readonly string[]` as two members, since they are two types. An object field gains `readonly` (written only when true) for `readonly name: T`, and `indexKey` (written only for an index signature) for `[name: K]: T`: such a field is `name` the key parameter, `propType` the value and `indexKey` the key type. An index signature is not a prop, so everything that reads an object's fields as props, keys or lookup targets skips a field with `indexKey` (`is_member`), and a props type with only an index signature lists no props. `ref` gains `name`, the React type as written without `React.` (`Ref`, `RefObject`, `ForwardedRef`, `MutableRefObject`, `RefCallback`, `LegacyRef`, `ElementRef`), and `raw_string` prints `RefObject<T>` rather than `Ref<T>`. `declare const X: readonly ["a", "b"]` is still read as the tuple (`record_declared_value_type` peels the operator). The Vite plugin and the Storybook addon read version 4 only, and an old parse cache is discarded once (`CACHE_SCHEMA_VERSION` in `cache.rs`). The TypeScript 7.1 backend sets none of them: `crates/semantic/src/convert.rs` builds every object field as a plain, writable member and has no `readonly` or ref conversion. Not carried: `readonly` on a component's own props (a `readonly label: string` member of a props interface), which no consumer reads yet. A collapsed inheritance layer (`docs/architecture/resolver-type-resolution.md`, Collapse) also names the props it holds: `InheritedLayer.members`, written as the id of a list in `nameLists` (so a source shared by many components is written once) and only for a layer that has members. It is an addition a reader can ignore, so it did not move `SCHEMA_VERSION`; a reader that wants to know whether a prop collapsed away or is absent reads it. Version 5 (ADR 0026, slice S1) writes a tuple's elements as `{ "type", "label"?, "modifier"?: "optional" | "rest" | "variadic" }` instead of bare types, which a version 4 reader would misread, and adds the kinds `bigintLiteral`, `uniqueSymbol`, `enumMember`, `templateLiteral`, `callable`, `recursive` and `truncated`; it drops the `templateLiteral` opaque reason, since a template that does not expand is now kept as its pattern. `degradedBy` gains `truncated`. The shapes and which of them the structural path fills are in `types-and-output-contract.md` (Schema version 5). The Vite plugin, the Storybook addon and the oracle read version 5 only, and the parse cache moved to version 33. Version 6 (trail 276) adds the kind `bigint` for the primitive, which version 5 wrote as the name `bigint`; a version 5 reader would show the primitive as a type called `bigint` that it cannot find. Both the structural path and the checker converter write it, and the readers (Vite plugin, Storybook addon, oracle, scripts) read version 6 only. The parse cache moved to version 34.

